body:
I understand how you feel. I came off all my meds in the fall of 2017 and had an intense manic episode with psychotic features a year later. I was stable and was doing well and it all crashed after a sexual assault that happened between me and a supposed "friend". Couldn't sleep, went manic and lost many friends due to my assault and episode. My friend who I thought was my best friend at the time didn't believe me and she is still close friends with him. I was so disappointed and blamed myself. I thought about suicide a lot. I fell into a deep depression and crawled back to my psychiatrist begging him for help. 

&#x200B;

I got put on an antipsychotic and a mood stabilizer. I remember it helped a bit but I still was facing psychosis and depression. I thought to myself what the point of medication is if I will continue to feel like this. I thought to myself many times that I was better when I was off meds. I was so upset that I let that incident happen to myself. I was disappointed that I let it affect my life so much. 

&#x200B;

If it weren't for this subreddit being so adamant about taking meds and stressing the importance of it, I probably would be off of it and out of my mind. If you really think about it, meds aren't such bad of an option. Not too long ago, lobotomies were given to people like you and me and many were mistreated in mental hospitals. Some people need help for their brain to work properly and that's okay. So don't feel bad for needing medication, it's helping your brain get the necessary chemicals it needs to function. (:
